    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "haha that's funny. but i'm really worried about the nutrients. you mentioned nitrogen deficiency—is there something else that could be \"stealing\" the nutrients or making them unavailable to the plant? like, is there something hidden in the soil?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Good instinct — yes, there are a few \"hidden thieves\" in soil. Here are the main suspects:\n\n**Nutrient lockout from pH:** If your soil is too acidic (below ~5.5) or too alkaline (above ~7), nitrogen, phosphorus, and micronutrients get chemically \"tied up\" and the roots can't absorb them, even if they're physically present. A cheap soil test kit will tell you your pH. Tomatoes like 6.0–6.8.\n\n**Compacted or waterlogged soil:** Soggy, dense soil literally drowns roots, and suffocated roots can't take up nutrients no matter how much is there. If water pools after watering, this could be your culprit.\n\n**Nutrient competition:** Weeds, or even planting tomatoes too close together, means roots fighting over the same supply.\n\n**Depleted soil:** If you've grown heavy feeders like tomatoes in the same spot for years without adding organic matter, the nitrogen may genuinely be gone.\n\nThe fix: get a soil test, amend pH if needed, top-dress with compost, and rotate where you plant tomatoes next year.\n\nAnd honestly, the \"hidden thief\" framing isn",
      "source": "target",
