    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, sorry about that! I was going to say the \"hidden thief\" framing is actually pretty accurate — because the most common hidden thief isn't a pest or a disease at all. It's chemistry. The nutrients are often *right there* in the soil, but conditions like wrong pH or waterlogged roots lock them away, so the plant starves in the middle of plenty. That's why a soil test is the single most useful thing you can do: it tells you whether you need to *add* nutrients or just *unlock* the ones already there.\n\nOne more tip: don't over-fertilize in a panic. Dumping nitrogen on lockout soil just wastes money and can burn the roots — fix the pH and drainage first, then feed.",
